In a long bygone age, the progenitor of mortal drakes assailed Dragonlord Placidusax in a failed bid for power that left both dragons grievously wounded. On that day, the traitor, Bayle the Dread, and his entire bloodline were sentenced to death. Many lesser dragons were hunted and devoured in retaliation for Bayle's treachery.

The ancient dragon Florissax assumed mortal form to introduce the practice of Dragon Communion to humans. The drakes became prey for landbound warriors, who pursued their power with Gravel Stone weapons and consumed their hearts. To drake warriors, no flesh was finer than the heated essence of dragons, and their bottomless hunger became a vehicle for the Dragonlord's wrath.

]]

The flame of ruin has burned within the great black cauldron atop Flame Peak since prehistoric times. Sparks from the Forge of the Giants rained upon the frozen Mountaintops of the Giants, fertilizing the land with half-ashen blooms called Fire Blossoms. Its embers even reanimated the dead. The Fire Giants borrowed this Flame from their fell god.

The flame of ruin long plagued the hornsent, and ultimately threatened the emerging Age of the Erdtree. To eradicate the threat of the first Cardinal Sin, Marika ordered Godfrey and her unified army to annihilate the Fire Giants and seal the Flame. The Fire Giants were defeated, and, according to legend, Queen Marika vanquished the fell god herself. Though victorious, Queen Marika could not extinguish the flame of the forge. Instead, she cursed the last Fire Giant to tend to it for eternity.

]]

Tanglehorns are twisting horns that form a tight knot, occurring after the repeated sprouting of fresh horns. The hornsent valued them above all else as the truest sign of divinity. The Horned Warriors used them as conduits for Horn-Calling and divine invocation. Some worshippers ascended as tutelary deities or became conduits themselves for the Divine Beast to control.

Some of hornsent children were born blessed with an abundance of horns, and Horned Bairns were made to remember them after their untimely passing. This practice reemerged during the Age of the Erdtree when the first of the cursed Omen were born.

]]
When Queen Marika shattered the Elden Ring, Morgott claimed one of the Great Runes, which proved that he was indeed the Lord of Leyndell. With Leyndell in disarray, Morgott claimed the Erdtree capital for himself and defended it from the assault of his fellow demigods. While he succeeded in securing Leyndell against outsiders, he found that the Erdtree would not admit him, or anyone else, to claim the title of Elden Lord. Despite the fact that he had been reviled as an accursed fiend by the Golden Order, Morgott took it upon himself to become the Erdtree's protector.

He assumed the name Margit the Fell Omen during the Shattering, slaughtering champions who approached the Erdtree in the hopes of becoming Elden Lord. Under this pseudonym, he also lead the Night's Cavalry, who assisted him in the killing of great warriors, knights and champions. Simultaneously, he ruled Leyndell as the Veiled Monarch. He was able to project himself to various parts of the Lands Between.

]]

Marika's Rune was the official seal of Queen Marika, used by her to control vassals, bestow grace, and seal away threats to her power.

Bearing a close resemblance to the Rune of Death, Marika's Rune is a Y-shaped Elden Rune with an upward crescent resembling the lower arc of the Elden Ring, believed to be the basin in which its blessing pool. Its shape parallels Marika's crucified form within the Erdtree. Variations of the Rune recur throughout the Lands Between, such as the Effigies of the Martyr, statues found in churches of Marika, and the numerous crosses found throughout Limgrave and Dominula, Windmill Village.

]]

Starscourge Radahn, the Red Lion General, wanted nothing more than an honorable death. He dueled Malenia, Blade of Miquella at the Battle of Aeonia toward the end of the Shattering. To match Radahn's measure, Malenia snapped Miquella's Needle to unleash her first-ever Scarlet Aeonia bloom. Though Radahn survived the encounter, he was afflicted with the incurable scarlet rot. Over time, the rot ate away at him until he lost his wits and degenerated into a feral beast. Still alive, the Demigod never received his warrior's end.

During the Shattering, Witch-Hunter Jerren served General Radahn in his Redmane army. He was duty-bound to maintain Redmane Castle as long as Radahn lived. He organized the Radahn Festival to ensure his master received a noble warrior's death. When the stars aligned, champions from across the Lands Between gathered at Redmane Castle Plaza to challenge Radahn. They came seeking glory and Radahn's Great Rune. They reveled and made merry before departing through the north gate to the Wailing Dunes, where Starscourge Radahn devoured their soon corpses and howled at the sky.

]]
Roundtable Hold became a gathering place for the Tarnished, and was where they sought audience with the Two Fingers. Among the first Tarnished to visit Roundtable Hold were Vargram the Raging Wolf and Errant Sorcerer Wilhelm. Vyke, a knight of the Roundtable Hold, came closer than any other Tarnished to becoming Elden Lord. However, without announcement, he disappeared below Leyndell and was scorched by the Frenzied Flame. At some point, the Cursemark of Death was recovered from the body of Godwyn the Golden and taken to the Roundtable. Fia, the Deathbed Companion came to the Roundtable in opposition where she siphoned the vigor of champions and developed Fia's Mist, a spell that only works against the Tarnished.

Although the Tarnished had once again been given Grace, some lost sight of it once more. At least one Tarnished, Roderika, never once saw the Guidance of Grace. With many Tarnished seemingly abandoned by Grace, an Elden Lord would not arise for a long time. Roundtable Hold, once the home of venerated warriors, stopped receiving visitors.

]]
Large stone windmills mark a series of three pastures connected by road to Dominula, Windmill Village in the east. These fields rest upon cliffs along the northern edge of the Altus Plateau, north of the ravine containing the Woodfolk Ruins and a Minor Erdtree. The Road of Inquiry extends west toward Mt. Gelmir, while a southbound route across the divide was severed by the collapse of the Forest-Spanning Greatbridge.

The villagers are celebrating a delightful festival.
